    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 **261 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` flag on the owner response: `true` when more than 80 owners have already been created today, otherwise `false`.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field (and its setter).\n\n2. **`OwnerBulkSignup.java`** (new helper) \u2014 Encapsulates the read-time derivation: counts owners sharing a given `registrationDate` and returns `true` when that count exceeds the threshold of `80`. This mirrors the same per-day accumulation path used by `EnsureDailyOwnerLimit` (the daily create-limit rule), keeping the two rules consistent.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Marked `bulkSignupWarning` as `ignore = true` on `toOwnerDto`, since it's not derivable from the `Owner` entity alone (it needs a repository count) and is set explicitly by the responders.\n\n4. **`RespondWithOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `RespondWithOwnerUpdated`** \u2014 Each now injects `OwnerRepository`, maps the owner to a DTO, and sets `bulkSignupWarning` from `OwnerBulkSignup.isWarning(...)` keyed on the owner's own `registrationDate`. This covers the GET, POST (create), and PUT (update) single-owner responses.\n\nThe design keeps it a purely read-time derivation (like the existing `membershipTier` and `locality` fields), so both the create response and subsequent GETs report a consistent value. The acceptance test `coreNoBulkWarningUnderThreshold` passes (a fresh create \u2192 `false`), and the `>80` branch reuses the daily-limit rule's counting logic.\n\nOne design decision worth flagging: I keyed \"today\" on each owner's stored `registrationDate` (the adjusted business day), counting inclusively, so a GET of the 81st owner created on a given day reports `true`. This matches the daily-limit rule's accumulation path exactly and is deterministic per owner. If the spec intends \"already created *before* this one\" (strict off-by-one, so the 81st create shows `false` and only the 82nd shows `true`), that would be a one-line change \u2014 let me know and I'll adjust.",
